feedback.message:
DEFECT 2: Verdict text is nonsensical for false-start results.

In render() at the "result" phase, the verdict is calculated unconditionally:
  let verdict = ms < 200 ? "Too fast — were you guessing?" : ...

For a false start, last.ms is null. In JavaScript, null < 200 evaluates to true (null coerced to 0), so the verdict always shows "Too fast — were you guessing?" for false starts. This is displayed in the subtitle below the "False start!" title.

Fix: Guard the verdict calculation to only run for valid trials:
  let verdict = "";
  if (!last.falseStart) {
    verdict = ms < 200 ? "Too fast — were you guessing?" :
              ms < 250 ? "Quick reflexes!" :
              ms < 350 ? "Solid reaction." : "Take your time next time.";
  }

DEFECT 3: Average shows 0ms when all trials are false starts.

In renderDone():
  const valid = results.filter(r => !r.falseStart).map(r => r.ms);
  const avg = valid.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / (valid.length || 1);

When all 5 trials are false starts, valid is []. reduce returns 0 (initial value). valid.length || 1 is 1. So avg = 0 / 1 = 0. The summary displays "Average: 0ms" which is incorrect.

Fix: Show dash when there are no valid trials:
  const avg = valid.length ? Math.round(valid.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / valid.length) : null;
And update the template to show dash for null avg.
